"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nAs the second full-length novel of , it is a work that focuses more on the most classic proposition of mystery: how did they kill.  \n\nThe two core keys that penetrate this work are the concepts of Imaginary Solution and Salvation.  \nImaginary Solution symbolizes a , namely a perfect crime.  \nOn the other hand, Salvation holds the contradictory meanings of both a merciful act of sparing a life and a cold preparation process for certain judgment.  \n\nIt seems to be a work that shakes the conventions of this genre in that it's about the  rather than the effort to kill.  \n\nThere are characters who approach a single goal, the truth, in different ways.  \n\nPersonally, I think it's one of the most bold and ingenious tricks."